From: PortareOS <hello@portare.org>
Subject: [PATCH] ASoC: q6apm-dai: the period floor as a parameter

1073 replaced upstream's fixed 480-frame step with 10 ms of frames at
the stream's own rate, and said so plainly: "Every period is still at
least 10 ms and a multiple of 32 frames, both of which 480 already was,
so the DSP sees nothing shorter or less aligned than it does at 48 kHz
today."

That was the safe choice, and it means the DSP's real limit has never
been tested. Upstream's only word on it is the comment it deleted -
"setup 10ms latency to accommodate DSP restrictions" - with no
restriction named and no reference. Both numbers are ours, and 320
frames at 32 kHz is a consequence of them, not of the hardware.

They gate everything above: PipeWire's quantum cannot be smaller than
what the driver will hand the DSP, so the 8 ms audio setting sits on
top of this and not on anything measured. Before asking whether
PipeWire is the wrong thing in the path, the floor under it has to be
known.

So: parameters, defaulting to the current values, so nothing changes
until somebody sets one.

    period_min_ms   10   the floor in milliseconds
    period_align    32   frames the period is rounded up to

The rule runs from hw_params, so a new value takes effect on the next
PCM open - no unbind, no reload, no reboot. Set it, restart the stream,
measure.

What to expect, so the result can be wrong: if the DSP takes 2 or 3 ms
periods then the floor under the whole audio path is far lower than
anything here assumed. If it refuses below 10, upstream's comment was
right, 8 ms is close to the hardware, and the question is closed. A
value the DSP dislikes should surface as xruns or a failed prepare
rather than silence, but that is a prediction and not a promise, so
sweep downwards and keep pcm-flags and pw-top in view.

--- a/sound/soc/qcom/qdsp6/q6apm-dai.c
+++ b/sound/soc/qcom/qdsp6/q6apm-dai.c
@@ -396,9 +396,25 @@ static int q6apm_dai_trigger(struct snd_soc_component *component,
  * whole 32-frame blocks: 480 at 48 kHz as before, 320 at 32 kHz, 448 at
  * 44.1 kHz. Every period stays at least 10 ms and a multiple of 32 frames,
  * both of which 480 already was.
+ *
+ * Both numbers are this fork's, not the DSP's. Upstream said only "setup 10ms
+ * latency to accommodate DSP restrictions" and named no restriction, so what
+ * the DSP will actually take below 10 ms has never been established - and it
+ * is the floor under everything above, since PipeWire cannot run a quantum
+ * smaller than the period the driver hands over. Hence parameters rather than
+ * constants: the rule reads them from hw_params, so a new value applies on the
+ * next PCM open.
  */
-#define Q6APM_PERIOD_MIN_MS	10
-#define Q6APM_PERIOD_ALIGN	32
+static unsigned int period_min_ms = 10;
+static unsigned int period_align = 32;
+
+module_param(period_min_ms, uint, 0644);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(period_min_ms,
+	"period floor in ms handed to the DSP (default 10, upstream's implied value)");
+
+module_param(period_align, uint, 0644);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(period_align,
+	"frames the period is rounded up to (default 32)");
 
 static int q6apm_dai_rule_period_step(struct snd_pcm_hw_params *params,
 				      struct snd_pcm_hw_rule *rule)
@@ -406,14 +427,20 @@ static int q6apm_dai_rule_period_step(struct snd_pcm_hw_params *params,
 	struct snd_interval *size = hw_param_interval(params, rule->var);
 	unsigned int step, min, max;
 	struct snd_interval t;
+	unsigned int ms = period_min_ms, align = period_align;
 
 	/* Until the rate is a single value there is no frame length to step by. */
 	if (!snd_interval_single(rate))
 		return 0;
 
-	step = roundup(DIV_ROUND_UP(snd_interval_value(rate) * Q6APM_PERIOD_MIN_MS,
-				    1000), Q6APM_PERIOD_ALIGN);
+	/* A zero from either would divide by nothing or round to nothing. */
+	if (!ms)
+		ms = 1;
+	if (!align)
+		align = 1;
+
+	step = roundup(DIV_ROUND_UP(snd_interval_value(rate) * ms, 1000), align);
 
 	min = size->min + (size->openmin ? 1 : 0);
 	max = size->max - (size->openmax ? 1 : 0);
